dedos4@wsintt12.info.win.tue.nl (Boudewijn Wayers) writes:
>nino@vmars.tuwien.ac.at (Marinos Yannikos) writes:
>
>>Isn't there a substance able to dissolve gold? I can't remember what
>>it's called, sorry... Would make a nice potion, perhaps giving you 'bad
>>breath' (like the fountain) as an alternative use (since Midas would be
>>unique).
>
>There is indeed. It is called a (fluid) metal called mercury and you
>can find it in most thermometers. Watch out when you break a
>thermometer! Take off you rings first, because they will dissolve if
>they touch mercury.

Bzzt. Mercury does *not* dissolve gold. It *will* amalgamate with it (for
any other metal than mercury the word would be "alloy", but mercury's
different for some reason) to give a silvery *solid* amalgam called "white
gold" perhaps familiar to those who've read about Covenant... your rings
*won't* dissolve - but they will turn silvery quite nicely.

The fluid Marinos is thinking of is "aqua regia", mixed nitric and
hydrochloric acid (neither of which will do the trick by itself).

>And, by the way, mercury would NOT make a nice potion. It is highly
>poisonous. A single drop is enough to kill a human. Thus, it would not
>give you bad breath, but no breath at all anymore.

Mmmm... it's poisonous, yes; worse, its *vapors* are cumulatively poisonous
and it evaporates well at room temperature. If you spill it, call your local
chemistry department to get the lowdown on cleaning it up, please.

